\end{align*} Since the bases are equal, their exponents must be equal. Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
x+4 &= 3x \\
\Rightarrow & x &= -4.
\end{align*} The answer is: $-4$.
Alternatively, since both sides of the equation are powers of 5, we can write them as powers of 5 raised to a common exponent. Then, we can equate the exponents and solve for $x$. We get \begin{align*}
(x+4) &= 3x \\
\Rightarrow & x &= -4.
