question:
For how many positive integral values of $a$ is it true that $x = 2$ is the only positive integer solution of the system of inequalities $$
\begin{cases}
2x>3x-3\\
3x-a>-6
\end{cases}
$$

output:
First, we can write each term as a product of two square roots:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{5}} &= \frac{\sqrt{2} \cdot \sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{5} \cdot \sqrt{5}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{10}}{5}.
\end{align*}
Similarly,
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{6}} &= \frac{\sqrt{3} \cdot \sqrt{6}}{\sqrt{6} \cdot \sqrt{6}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{18}}{6}.
\end{align*}
And
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{4}}{\sqrt{7}} &= \frac{\sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{7}}{\sqrt{7} \cdot \sqrt{7}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{28}}{7}.
\end{align*}
Now, let's multiply these fractions together. The numerator is:
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt{10})(\sqrt{18})(\sqrt{28}) &= (\sqrt{10})(\sqrt{9 \cdot 2})(\sqrt{4 \cdot 7}) \\
&= (\sqrt{10})(\sqrt{9})(\sqrt{2})(\sqrt{4})(\sqrt{7}) \\
&= (10)(3)(2)(2)(\sqrt{7}) \\
