We have:
\begin{align*}
9x - 4y &= 30 \quad (1) \\
7x + y &= 11 \quad (2)
\end{align*}

To eliminate one variable, we can multiply equation (2) by 4 so that the coefficients of \(y\) in both equations are opposites:

\begin{align*}
4(7x + y) &= 4(11) \\
28x + 4y &= 44 \quad (3)
\end{align*}

Now, subtract equation (1) from equation (3):

\begin{align*}
(28x + 4y) - (9x - 4y) &= 44 - 30 \\
28x + 4y - 9x + 4y &= 14 \\
19x + 8y &= 14
\end{align*}

Next, we can express \(y\) in terms of \(x\):

\begin{align*}
8y &= 14 - 19x \\
y &= \frac{14 - 19x}{8}
\end{align*}

Substitute this expression for \(y\) into equation (2):

\begin{align*}
7x + \left(\frac{14 - 19x}{8}\right) &= 11 \\
56x + 14 - 19x &= 88 \\
37x = 74 \\
x &= 2
\end{align*}

Now substitute \(x = 2\) back into the expression for \(y\):

\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{14 - 19(2)}{8} \\
&= \frac{14 - 38}{8} \\
&= \frac{-24}{8} \\
&= -3
\end{align*}

