Let $p$ be the price of a pencil, and let $n$ be the price of a pen. We are given that
\begin{align*}
5p + n &= 2.50 \quad \text{(1)} \\
p + 2n &= 1.85 \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}We can solve this system of equations using substitution or elimination. Here, we will use elimination.

First, multiply equation (1) by 2 to make the coefficients of $p$ in both equations equal:
\begin{align*}
10p + 2n &= 5.00 \quad \text{(3)} \\
p + 2n &= 1.85 \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}

Now subtract equation (2) from equation (3):
\begin{align*}
(10p + 2n) - (p + 2n) &= 5.00 - 1.85 \\
9p &= 3.15 \\
p &= \frac{3.15}{9} \\
p &= 0.35
\end{align*}

Substitute the value of $p$ back into equation (2) to find $n$:
\begin{align*}
0.35 + 2n &= 1.85 \\
2n &= 1.85 - 0.35 \\
2n &= 1.50 \\
n &= \frac{1.50}{2} \\
n &= 0.75
\end{align*}

Now, calculate the cost of two pencils and one pen:
\begin{align*}
2p + n &= 2(0.35) + 0.75 \\
&= 0.70 + 0.75 \\
&= 1.45
\end{align*}

Therefore, the cost of two pencils and one pen is $\boxed{1.45}$ dollars. The answer is 1.45.

