First, let's consider the function \( j(x) = \max\{f(x), g(x), h(x)\} \). Since \( f(x) \), \( g(x) \), and \( h(x) \) are linear functions, they can take any value in a certain range. The maximum value among them will be the highest possible value, which is the largest among \( f(x) \), \( g(x) \), and \( h(x) \).

Similarly, the minimum value among them will be the lowest possible value, which is the smallest among \( f(x) \), \( g(x) \), and \( h(x) \).

The graph shows that the maximum value of \( j(x) \) is at \( x = -2 \) because \( f(-2) \) is the highest, followed by \( g(-2) \), then \( h(-2) \), and finally \( f(-2) \) again. Similarly, the minimum value of \( j(x) \) is at \( x = 2 \) because \( g(2) \) is the highest, followed by \( h(2) \), then \( f(2) \), and finally \( g(2) \) again.

Therefore, the length of the graph of \( y = k(x) \) from \( x = -3.5 \) to \( x = 3.5 \) is the distance between \( x = -2 \) and \( x = 2 \), which is \( 2 - (-2) = 4 \).

Thus, the length \( \ell \) is 4, so \( \ell^2 = 4^2 = 16 \).

The answer is \(\boxed{16}\).
